Found while doing #38, which fixed the same hazard in Role/BoardDiscovery (Path::Tiny) and Role/SyncLifecycle (Carp).

`App::karr::Role::ClaimTimeout` and `App::karr::Role::TaskMutation` both say `use Time::Piece;`, which installs its localtime/gmtime replacements into the role package -- and a Moo::Role composes every sub in its package into its consumers, imports included. So `$cmd->localtime` and `$cmd->gmtime` are callable on every mutating command class.

Arguably worse than the #38 cases: these shadow builtins. A future `sub localtime` or an attribute of that name on a command class fights an inherited Time::Piece export, and the failure would look like a core function misbehaving.

Same cure as #38, already the house rule in Role/Output, Role/BoardDiscovery and Role/SyncLifecycle: load with an explicit empty import list and qualify at the call site. Note Time::Piece is not a drop-in for that -- its whole point is replacing the builtins, so the call sites need `Time::Piece::localtime(...)` / `->strptime` style rather than the bare form, and each site must be checked for whether it wanted the overridden version or the builtin. That is why #38 did not sweep it in passing.

Not urgent: latent, nothing calls them today. t/121-role-import-leakage.t records both in an explicit KNOWN_LEAK allow-list, so this does not rot silently and a NEW leak fails the test. Delete the entries there when this is done.
Done, uncommitted in the working tree (parallel lane #108 holds Cmd/Context.pm and Foundation/* in the same tree).

Role/ClaimTimeout.pm: use Time::Piece; -> use Time::Piece (); Two call sites, decided separately. ->strptime (line ~100) was already a qualified class method and needed no import. The gmtime in _claim_expired wants the OVERLOADED one: it is subtracted from the parsed Time::Piece stamp, and the builtin returns a string in that context, so it is now Time::Piece::gmtime(). Proven, not assumed -- reverting just that call to the bare builtin turns t/72-claim-timeout.t red (2 of 4 subtests), which is exactly the silent date bug the ticket warned about.

Role/TaskMutation.pm: use Time::Piece; deleted outright. The role never asks for the time -- the lifecycle stamps are written by App::karr::Task::update_timestamps, which loads its own. The import was pure leak.

Other localtime/gmtime sites read, none changed: Task.pm, BoardStore.pm, Cmd/Pick, Cmd/Move, Cmd/Edit, Cmd/Handoff all want Time::Piece objects and import into their own class package (self-inflicted only, the smaller problem #38 already noted). ActivityLog.pm:208 is the codebase counter-example and is correct as is -- POSIX::strftime with the BUILTIN gmtime() list, no Time::Piece in that file. Foundation.pm and Cmd/Context.pm belong to the #108 lane, untouched.

t/121-role-import-leakage.t: %KNOWN_LEAK is now empty (both entries deleted, with a note saying #105 emptied it). Added two subtests in the #38 style: the role packages no longer hold gmtime/localtime while Time::Piece stays loaded, and a MutationConsumer test class has no ->gmtime/->localtime but still has run_batch/update_task_guarded/apply_status_change/check_claim. Mutation-checked: restoring use Time::Piece; in ClaimTimeout fails 3 of 5 subtests.

Verified latent, not behavioural: nothing anywhere calls ->gmtime/->localtime (grep over lib/ bin/ t/), and a role-composed sub does not override a builtin for bare calls in the consumer -- only method calls and fully qualified ones see it. So no behaviour changed.

